WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:04.280
Can a Hebrew speaker understand an Arabic speaker?

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:12.400
Well, the short answer is "No".
The long answer is "kinda, sorta, maybe, but not really, no."

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:23.440
The question of the day is: 
"How similar are Hebrew and Arabic?"

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:28.400
As someone who has studied both languages,
I get asked this question by quite a few people.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:35.000
And it's a good question, it makes me think, because I'm not quite sure, but I'll give you my best answer.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:40.060
Basically, Hebrew and Arabic are very closely related languages,

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:44.100
and if you study both, that will become very apparent. You'll see tons of similarities.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.800
And if you know one well already, 
that will help you learn the other much more easily.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.520
But at the same time, 
they are not mutually comprehensible.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.360
That means, if you speak one and not the other,

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.700
then you won't be able to communicate with someone 
who speaks the other language. So they're not....

00:01:02.879 --> 00:01:06.859
You can't... you can't communicate 
as though they're the same language, it won't work.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:12.220
OK, so let's talk about some things that are kind of 
similar, and I'll throw in some differences as I go.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:19.040
And then I'll talk about how they're... not really useful 
as a communication tool with each other.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:27.080
OK, so, they're similar in terms of the verbal system, 
and in terms of how you create words in the language.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.720
So, in both Hebrew and Arabic, 
they have a system of roots.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:35.000
Now, those roots are usually three consonant letters.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:41.420
And you take those three consonant letters, and you put them 
into kind of templates that help you create a word.

00:01:41.429 --> 00:01:49.109
So the templates are patterns. They're like... 
a set of... a combination of vowels and affixes.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:53.580
They could be a prefix, or a suffix or even an infix inside the word.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:56.900
So, let's take a three-letter root 
and I'll give you an example,

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:02.020
In Hebrew, you've got the root "K", "T", "V".
That's K-T-V, if you romanise it.

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:06.840
And in Arabic, you've got "K", "T", "B" :
K-T-B, if you romanise it.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:10.780
Already, you can see there's a similarity,
that root is almost the same.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.520
The "B" and the "V" are different,
but those sounds are equivalent.

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.440
So if you put them into the templates you can change it.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:23.600
Let's use a verb conjugation template in the past tense, this is the simplest structure in both languages.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:27.020
So in Hebrew if you say "I wrote", 
you would say "katavti".

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:32.120
In Arabic, you would say "katabtu", 
or in a dialect, maybe "katabt".

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:38.220
In Hebrew, for the second person masculine.
For "you": you would say "katavta".

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:41.980
In Arabic, "katabta".
So you can see that's quite similar.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:48.520
For the feminine second person singular,
In Hebrew, you would say "katavt".

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:54.360
In Arabic, you would say "katabti".
OK, so you can see there, there's a similarity here,

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:58.750
in the way that you put the root 
into these templates to conjugate the verbs.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:02.980
And you can see that the conjugations are kind of similar,

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.400
the templates are kind of similar, but they're not exactly the same, and there could be confusion.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:15.560
Like.... for... for the one I said for...
in Hebrew, it's "katavti", that's "I wrote",

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:20.440
but "katabti" in Arabic means "you wrote",
it's when it's spoken to a female,

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:22.700
So you can see there could be a confusion there.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.160
You might misunderstand 
and think they're talking about someone else.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:30.280
Right, but you can see that's the same kind of system,

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:34.320
and that, that kind of system of templates 
is used to create nouns, to create adjectives,

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.100
all kinds of words, just the templates are different,

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:41.920
and have different affixes attached 
to them... and different vowels.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:45.720
So that's one thing is that system of
roots and templates: It's the same in both.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.720
So if you learned that once, for one language,

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:53.600
it's just getting that concept down really helps you with the second language that uses that same system,

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:58.220
because it's a bit mind-bending at first
to adjust to that root and template system

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:02.140
Another way that they're similar is 
in terms of just a lot of vocabulary.

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:06.240
You already saw that that one root: 
the "k-t-v" and the "k-t-b".

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:09.980
So there's a lot of vocabulary that's the same, 
it's not the majority of words though,

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:15.060
it's not like 80% or something like that
it's... I don't know the exact percentage,

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:18.760
I would guess that it's about a third.

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:22.989
That's just off the top of my head 
from my studying experience: a third.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:28.740
Some of the words are very similar 
or almost the same, and some are...

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:35.740
some are clearly related, if you examine the words, 
but you might not notice that just from hearing them,

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:40.840
because the phonology of the languages has 
become different, due to the language's history.

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:43.660
It's similar in French and Italian.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:49.020
[In] French and Italian if you read two sentences,
equivalent sentences, side by side,

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:52.580
you can look at them and you can say,
"Oh this word must be the same as this word,"

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:56.720
"This one must be the same as this one,"
because the... the words look kind of similar.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.880
They sound different and the spelling is different,
but you can look at them and see that they're the same.

00:05:01.039 --> 00:05:03.639
They have the same meaning because they look similar.

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:08.420
There are a lot of words like that... like they're... 
kind of cognates, but they're slightly different.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:12.760
So I'll give you some examples of both types,
like the ones that are very similar.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:16.620
"Yad" in Hebrew and Arabic, that means hand.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:20.340
"Dam" in Hebrew and Arabic that means blood.

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:25.640
Right, there's a lot of words that are similar like that, 
or almost the same like that,

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:28.820
some that you can figure out quite easily, 
like the pronouns, like...

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:33.200
I is "ani" in Hebrew, or "ana" in Arabic.

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:38.580
Or in Hebrew "you" for a man is "ata", in Arabic it's "anta".
Right?

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:43.520
he is "hu" in Hebrew, it's "huwa" in Arabic.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:49.420
Right, now, it might depend on the dialect too in Arabic, it's different in...from country to country.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:56.420
Another one: Kitchen in Hebrew: "mitbach", 
and in Arabic it's "matbach",

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.820
So, pretty close, 
just one vowel changes in the Arabic one,

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:04.800
Some that are a little bit more different 
but you can see the similarity,

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:08.360
especially if you know the function 
or meaning of the word, like this one:

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:12.700
"to you" or "for you". 
In hebrew is "lecha", but in Arabic it's "lak".

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:18.540
So if you look at the consonants, 
you can see "l/k" vs "l/ch",

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:20.940
Those might be the same, right?

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:27.140
"av" in Hebrew is "father", "ab" in Arabic. 
So again you see that "b" and the "v".

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:32.520
Another one: "ben" is "son" in Hebrew, 
"ibn" is "son" in Arabic,

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:36.360
so if you just look at the consonants, the "b" and the "n", you can say "Oh, those are related."

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:44.280
Another one "rosh", rosh means "head" in
Hebrew and "ra`s", "ra`s" means head in Arabic.

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:46.940
So "ra`s" and "rosh", those are clearly related.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:51.800
Another one: "tongue".
In Hebrew, "lashon", in Arabic, "lisaan",

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:55.740
"lisaan", so the "suh" and the "shuh", 
hmm, maybe there's a change there,

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:58.220
and also that the "oh" became an "ah" sound,

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:00.740
so there's some shifts in the sounds are
going on

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:04.100
"Dog" in Hebrew is "kelev", in Arabic it's "kalb",

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:08.660
so you can see there are a lot of words
that have a similar...similar root,

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:12.260
or they, they come from the same word,
but they've shifted and become different.

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:16.900
So there's a lot of words like that, that you wouldn't 
necessarily understand when you hear them.

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:18.620
Another one that's this...

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:23.420
There are some words that are the same, but 
they have a different meaning like "medina" or "madina"

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:28.460
in Hebrew, "medina" is state,...
like "Medinat Israel", the State of Israel,

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:30.860
but in Arabic, "madina" means a city.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:34.420
So they're both political entities, 
there's a connection in the meaning,

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:38.140
but it's not exactly the same meaning,
the meaning has become different.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:43.820
And there are also a lot of words that are just different, entirely, for whatever reason.

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:48.780
It's always kind of strange to discover words 
that are exactly the same in Hebrew and Arabic,

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:52.640
and then the next word in the sentence is entirely 
different, and you've never seen it before.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:58.620
So then why did this one become the same 
in both languages, but this one is different, why?

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:03.540
So, it makes it kind of interesting, I don't always 
know... usually don't know the answer, but yeah.

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:05.780
Anyway, there's a lot of different vocabulary.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:10.520
So you can see that they are quite related, and there's a lot of crossover,

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:16.820
but... you won't understand one, 
just by learning the other, right?

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:20.060
It will help you learn the other, 
if you make the effort though.

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:24.820
And if you take what you know and try to
apply it to the other language while learning.

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:29.640
So, let me give you an example 
of when I first went to Egypt in 1997,

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:33.200
I went there after having studied Hebrew
for a couple years already,

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:40.960
and I was visiting Israel, and I went to Egypt too, 
so I tried to use my Hebrew and apply it to Arabic,

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:46.180
because I only knew a little bit of Arabic, I didn't know 
enough to really have a conversation or whatever.

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:48.880
So I would try to use my very simple Arabic,

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:53.700
and insert Hebrew words into it, 
and I knew the difference in phonology,

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:58.340
so I would kind of shift and Arabise
my pronunciation of the Hebrew words,

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:04.700
and I was surprised by how often people understood me, and the people didn't speak Hebrew,

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:10.020
they weren't Hebrew speakers, but they understood, 
I guess they could they could hear the word,

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:14.520
and think of an equivalent or similar sounding word 
in Arabic, or they could hear the root,

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:19.260
and maybe that root was used in a different word 
in their Arabic dialect or whatever

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:22.580
So they could figure out 
what I was saying a lot of the time.

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:26.840
And they looked at me strangely, 
they thought maybe I was a weirdo,

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:30.480
or I remember they often asked me if I was from Lebanon.

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:37.020
So maybe they thought that my bizarre
Arabic pidgin language was Lebanese Arabic.

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:40.480
It seemed to be something like that,
they kept asking me if I was from Lebanon.

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:46.020
It happened like that in Jordan too,
I remember when I was in Jordan the same year...

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:55.540
that I managed to use some Hebrew words in situations 
where I was surprised that that it went across.

00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:58.640
So they understood a lot of my hebrew words when arabised them.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:02.400
So that's just an example 
of how the the languages cross over,

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:07.940
and you can apply one to the other, 
but they're generally not mutually comprehensible,

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:13.180
you have to make the effort to shift it, 
to change that knowledge into the other language.

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:18.060
So that's why they are two different languages,
not dialects of the same language.

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:21.540
Alright, so that's...
that's what I wanted to share with you.

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:25.880
Just my experience with how similar they are, 
to give you some similarities, differences,

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:29.000
[I] hope you understand a little bit better than before.

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:31.820
Alright, thank you for listening, good night!

